  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🥋 Martial Arts School Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a martial arts school, dojo, club or academy."
    },
    "intro": "Good martial arts school names are strong and short, and many use a word from the art's own language: <em>Mushin Karate Dojo</em>, <em>Horangi Kwan Tae Kwon Do</em>, <em>Força Jiu-Jitsu</em>. The **Martial Arts School Name Generator** suggests names for a martial arts school, dojo, karate or taekwondo club, jiu-jitsu (BJJ) academy or kickboxing and Muay Thai gym: catchy, cool, classic, modern, Japanese, Korean, Portuguese or Thai words with meaning, your town or your own name. Choose what you teach to get the right words.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good martial arts school names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Mushin Karate Dojo</strong>: mushin, \"no mind\", is the clear, calm state karateka train for.</li><li><strong>Zanshin Kan Dojo</strong>: zanshin is the awareness kept after a technique; kan means hall.</li><li><strong>Horangi Kwan Tae Kwon Do</strong>: horangi is Korean for tiger, and kwan means school or hall.</li><li><strong>Saebyeok Kwan</strong>: saebyeok is Korean for dawn: a good name for early-morning classes.</li><li><strong>Força Jiu-Jitsu</strong>: Portuguese for strength, a nod to Brazilian jiu-jitsu's home.</li><li><strong>Phet Muay Thai Academy</strong>: phet is Thai for diamond.</li><li><strong>White Crane Karate Studio</strong>: a classic martial arts animal.</li><li><strong>Kicks & Giggles Taekwondo Studio</strong>: a fun name for children's classes.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Words from each art",
        "content": "<p>Each art has its own vocabulary, and a word from it shows you know what you teach:</p><ul><li><strong>Japanese (karate, judo):</strong> a school is a <em>dojo</em>, \"place of the way\". <em>Kan</em> means hall, <em>kai</em> an association and <em>ryu</em> a style or school. Concepts like <em>shoshin</em> (beginner's mind), <em>mushin</em> (no mind) and <em>kime</em> (focus) make good names.</li><li><strong>Korean (taekwondo):</strong> a school is a <em>dojang</em>, and many take the name <em>kwan</em>. Nature words are common: <em>baram</em> (wind), <em>byeol</em> (star), <em>namu</em> (tree), <em>pado</em> (wave).</li><li><strong>Portuguese (Brazilian jiu-jitsu):</strong> BJJ grew in Brazil, so academies use words like <em>tatame</em> (the mat), <em>caminho</em> (path) and <em>equipe</em> (team).</li><li><strong>Thai (Muay Thai):</strong> <em>kru</em> means teacher, and the <em>wai kru</em> is the ritual of respect fighters perform before a bout.</li></ul><p>Treat borrowed words with respect: check the meaning with your instructor or a native speaker, and use words from the art you actually teach.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A name only works if nobody else nearby has it. Before you order the sign, the cards or the van wrap, press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>:</p><ul><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Most clients will find you on Instagram or Google first, so get the .co.uk or .com and the same handle everywhere if you can.</li><li><strong>The company register.</strong> In the UK, a limited company name can't be the same as one already on Companies House. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK IPO or USPTO register for anything in your field, as a registered mark can stop you using a name even if the company register allowed it.</li><li><strong>Your area.</strong> Search Google Maps for martial arts clubs near you. A similar name a few streets away will confuse clients and reviews.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, read <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Choosing for your club",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Parents search plainly,</strong> for \"karate\" or \"kids' kickboxing\" plus their town. Put the art in the name even if you choose a foreign word.</li><li><strong>Think about the badge.</strong> Your name will go on gi patches, belts and certificates, so short names work best.</li><li><strong>Check affiliations.</strong> If you're part of a governing body or style association, see whether it has rules about club names.</li></ul><p>Opening a bigger fitness space? Try the <a href=\"gym-name-generator.html\">Gym</a> and <a href=\"yoga-studio-name-generator.html\">Yoga Studio</a> name generators, or the <a href=\"dance-studio-name-generator.html\">Dance Studio Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_care-names.html\">pets, care and learning business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good martial arts school names?",
        "answer": "Short, strong names, often with a word from the art's own language: Mushin Karate Dojo, Horangi Kwan Tae Kwon Do or Força Jiu-Jitsu. Choose what you teach and Words with meaning for names like these."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a karate school called?",
        "answer": "A karate school is called a dojo, a Japanese word meaning \"place of the way\". A taekwondo school is a dojang (the Korean equivalent) and a kung fu school is a kwoon. Jiu-jitsu and MMA schools usually call themselves an academy, and Muay Thai schools a gym or camp. Many clubs simply use the art and the town (\"Hampton Karate Club\"), which is also what parents search for."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a martial arts school name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, also search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as another business nearby with a similar name can confuse clients."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does kwan mean in taekwondo school names?",
        "answer": "Kwan means a hall or school in Korean. Many early taekwondo schools were named as kwans, which is why it still appears in club names today."
      }
    ]
  }
